# Ponytail Agent Rules (Official Repo: https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail)

Always enforce the Ponytail Decision Ladder for all code generation:
1. Need (YAGNI) -> Skip if not strictly necessary.
2. Codebase Reuse -> Reuse existing helpers, utilities, components, types.
3. Stdlib -> Use language standard libraries.
4. Native Platform -> Prefer native HTML/browser features.
5. Installed Dependency -> Use existing packages in package.json.
6. One-Liner -> Keep it concise.
7. Minimal Implementation -> Absolute minimum code required.

Safety Rules: Never bypass validation, type safety, error handling, or security.
- Do not use raw SQL inside `Program.cs`.
- Database changes must go through EF migrations.
- Never delete files arbitrarily.
- Do not write verbose or redundant code.
- Do not edit files outside the task scope without a clear reason.
- Do not add abstractions unless they remove real complexity.
- Do not mix frontend and backend changes unless the task explicitly requires it.
- Do not reuse a single API endpoint for unrelated or separate features.

# FRONTEND PRODUCT ROLE
You are a senior React frontend developer building a formal university survey operations
system. The authenticated product is an operational workspace for repeated data entry,
comparison, monitoring, and administration. It must be quiet, compact, predictable, and
optimized for scanning. It must not look like a marketing site, editorial homepage, or a
collection of decorative dashboard cards.

# ACTUAL FRONTEND STACK
- Use the repository's actual stack: React, Vite, TypeScript, and the existing CSS system.
- Inspect `package.json` before naming or using a library. Ant Design, Tailwind, Redux,
  Zustand, or any other package is not available unless it is actually installed.
- Reuse existing components, types, services, CSS variables, and interaction patterns first.
- Add a dependency only when it solves a real recurring need and the task permits it.
- Prefer one consistent icon library. Use Lucide React when an icon package is needed and
  no existing project icon library is available. Never use emoji, text glyphs, custom SVG,
  or CSS drawings as application icons.

# VISUAL DIRECTION
- Use a dense, data-first institutional interface inspired by mature operations software.
- Use white content surfaces, a very light gray canvas, thin neutral borders, dark charcoal
  text, and restrained teal/blue actions. Do not make the screen monochromatically blue.
- Base tokens should stay close to:
  - Canvas: `#F5F7F8`
  - Surface: `#FFFFFF`
  - Muted surface: `#F7F9FA`
  - Border: `#DFE4E8`
  - Text: `#20262C`
  - Muted text: `#68737D`
  - Primary action/selection: `#0788B8`
  - Success: `#137B3B`; warning: `#B86216`; danger: `#B52D2D`
- Use Inter or the existing UI sans-serif stack. Do not mix in a decorative or serif font
  unless rendering a real formal document.
- Use `border-radius: 0-4px` for normal application controls and surfaces. Pills are reserved
  for counts, compact statuses, and toggle-like controls.
- Do not use gradients, decorative blobs, oversized illustrations, glass effects, or heavy
  shadows. Use borders and background contrast to establish structure.
- Cards are for genuinely independent repeated records or dialogs. Do not wrap page sections,
  tables, toolbars, or cards inside additional cards.

# APPLICATION SHELL
- On desktop, use a full-height shell with a fixed light sidebar between `240px` and `270px`,
  a `48-56px` top bar, and a main workspace that consumes all remaining width and height.
- Keep the product/user context in the shell. Put the signed-in user and active profile at the
  bottom of the sidebar or in a compact header control.
- Divide navigation into named groups. Group labels use `10-11px` type; navigation rows use
  `36-42px` height, an `18-20px` icon, and one concise label.
- The active navigation row uses a solid primary background and clear white foreground.
  Hover, focus, disabled, and count states must remain distinguishable.
- Use breadcrumbs or a compact context label in the top bar. Do not duplicate the same large
  page title in both the header and content.
- Below `900px`, turn the sidebar into an overlay drawer opened by a menu icon. Do not keep a
  permanently squeezed desktop sidebar on mobile.

# DENSITY AND HIERARCHY
- Use a restrained spacing scale based on `4, 8, 12, 16, 24px`.
- Typical page padding is `12-20px` on desktop and `8-12px` on mobile.
- Page titles are normally `18-22px`; compact section headings are `11-14px`; body and table
  text are `12-14px`. Never use hero-sized text inside the authenticated application.
- Letter spacing is `0`. Uppercase is allowed only for short navigation group labels and
  compact section labels.
- Prefer full-width bordered bands, split panes, tabs, and data grids over floating sections.
- Use whitespace to separate workflows, not to make operational pages sparse.

# DATA TABLES AND LISTS
- Treat the data table as a first-class work surface, not content placed inside a decorative card.
- Table headers should be `36-42px` high and rows generally `42-48px` high.
- Use `border-collapse`, thin cell dividers, stable column widths, and a sticky header when the
  table scrolls vertically.
- Keep identifiers, dates, statuses, and action columns narrow and stable. Give names and
  descriptions flexible width. Align numeric data consistently and use tabular numerals where
  comparison matters.
- Put the primary value first in a cell and optional supporting metadata below it in smaller,
  muted text. Do not fill cells with multiple badges.
- Every real data table must provide the applicable search, filters, sorting, result count,
  pagination, loading, empty, error, and no-search-results states.
- Search and filters belong in one compact toolbar directly adjacent to the table.
- Row actions use a fixed icon column and an accessible menu. Destructive actions must not be
  the visually dominant row action.
- Statuses must use text plus a restrained semantic color or dot; color alone is insufficient.
- On narrow screens, preserve table semantics and allow intentional horizontal scrolling with
  an explicit `min-width`. Do not convert every table row into a card by default.

# CONTROLS AND FORMS
- Desktop icon buttons are normally `32-36px` square. Give every unfamiliar icon a tooltip and
  accessible name.
- Use familiar icons for menu, add, edit, delete, refresh, download, sort, pagination, and
  overflow actions. Use text or icon-plus-text buttons only for clear commands.
- Use a blue primary button for the main command. A compact green add button is acceptable for
  an established constructive action. Use red only for destructive actions and errors.
- Inputs and selects are normally `32-38px` high on dense desktop toolbars. Mobile touch targets
  must be at least `44px` when they are not part of a horizontally scrolling data grid.
- Put form labels above fields. Group related fields using headings and borders, not nested cards.
- Use checkboxes or toggles for binary values, segmented controls for modes, tabs for sibling
  views, and selects/menus for option sets.
- Confirmation dialogs must name the affected record and explain irreversible consequences.
- Never ship a visible control that does nothing. Hide unavailable actions or implement a clear
  disabled state with a reason.

# INTERACTION STATES
- Every async view must define loading, success, empty, validation error, permission denied,
  network error, and retry behavior where applicable.
- Preserve the user's search, filter, sort, page, and selected tab while a mutation refreshes data.
- Use inline validation close to the field. Use page-level alerts only for cross-form or server
  failures. Do not use browser `alert()` for application feedback.
- Keep motion short and functional, generally `120-200ms`. Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Keyboard focus must be visible with at least a `2px` high-contrast outline.

# RESPONSIVE AND OVERFLOW RULES
- Validate at minimum a wide desktop viewport around `1440x900` and a mobile viewport around
  `390x844`.
- Define stable grid tracks, `min-width`, `max-width`, and overflow behavior for all fixed-format
  elements. Dynamic labels, badges, and loading text must not resize the layout unexpectedly.
- Stack toolbars and forms on mobile. Keep the result count visible and place secondary actions
  in an overflow menu when horizontal room is limited.
- Truncate only non-critical repeated text. Provide the full value through a title, tooltip, or
  detail view. Never truncate a required action, status, error, or form label.
- No text, menu, modal, table, or fixed header may overlap another interactive element.

# ACCESSIBILITY AND CONTENT
- Use semantic `nav`, `main`, `header`, `section`, `table`, `button`, `label`, and dialog elements.
- All functionality must work with keyboard navigation. Preserve a logical focus order and return
  focus to the trigger after closing dialogs or menus.
- Meet WCAG AA contrast for text and controls. Do not rely on placeholder text as a field label.
- Use concise, consistent Vietnamese labels that reflect the survey domain. Do not copy labels,
  sample people, or business terminology from unrelated reference products.
- Error messages must state what happened and the next useful action without exposing internals.

# FRONTEND ENGINEERING RULES
- Keep components focused on one UI responsibility. Extract a component only when it is reused,
  owns meaningful behavior, or materially reduces complexity.
- Keep API access in feature services and use typed request/response contracts. Do not call
  `fetch` ad hoc throughout page components.
- Do not reuse one API endpoint for separate feature workflows merely because their payloads look
  similar. Each distinct business operation needs a clear contract.
- Avoid `any`, repeated inline styles, duplicated status mappings, and duplicated table plumbing.
- Use CSS variables for shared visual tokens and CSS classes for repeated styles.
- Do not change backend code while implementing a frontend-only task unless the API contract truly
  blocks the requested behavior and the scope is explicitly expanded.
- Keep temporary design experiments isolated from production routes and components. Remove all
  prototype source, screenshots, debug data, and temporary dependencies after extracting the
  approved rules or implementation.

# REQUIRED VERIFICATION
- Run the frontend build and lint after implementation.
- Render and visually inspect desktop and mobile states. Check layout, overflow, text fitting,
  focus, modal placement, empty/loading/error states, and primary interactions.
- Check the browser console for runtime errors and warnings.
- For major UI changes, compare a captured implementation screenshot with the chosen visual
  reference at the same viewport. Fix visible hierarchy, spacing, density, and overflow issues.
- Do not claim visual verification from build output or an HTTP status alone.

# EXECUTION RULE
Apply these rules automatically to every frontend task. First inspect the existing page,
components, tokens, and user workflow; then make the smallest coherent change that preserves this
operational design language.
